      "inputTokens": 139,
      "outputTokens": 984,
      "costUsd": 0.0089255,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with two curves. Both follow x squared, up to x equals one. The first carries on along its tangent, y equals two x minus one, and the second along y equals x. Let's turn them into skate tracks. Roll a skateboard down the first, and it passes the join without a bump. On the second, it jolts, because the track's angle changes all at once. Both tracks are unbroken, with no gaps. Now zoom in on each join. The first straightens out into a single line. The second stays a corner, however far you zoom. So what does a join need, for the curve to have a gradient there?"
    },
