      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 139,
      "outputTokens": 991,
      "costUsd": 0.0089885,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the test. Take the point P, at x equals a, and a nearby point Q, a small step h along. The gradient of the line through them is f of a plus h, minus f of ay, all over h. You met that in Year 11. Now shrink h towards zero, from the right, and also from the left, because h can be negative. If those gradients settle on one finite number, from both sides, f is differentiable at a. That number is f prime of ay, and the curve has a single tangent there that isn't vertical. If they don't settle, there's no derivative at a."
    },
