      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 218,
      "outputTokens": 1536,
      "costUsd": 0.013933,
      "pronunciationText": "What if the gradients match, but the pieces don't meet? Here, x squared runs up to one, one. Then the right piece is y equals two x, which starts at height two. On a track, that's a step. Its gradient is two, the same as x squared at the join. A function is continuous at a when, from both sides, the curve heads to the value f of a itself. Here, from the right it heads to two, but f of one is one. So it isn't continuous. Now look at the secant from P, to a point on the right piece. The rise never drops below one, while the run shrinks to zero. So the gradient blows up, to twelve, then a hundred and two, and beyond. No derivative. This is why a function that's differentiable at a point must be continuous there. If the gradients settle on a finite number, the rise is that gradient times the run. So the rise shrinks to zero with the run, and the curve can't jump."
    },
