      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 1069,
      "costUsd": 0.0096925,
      "pronunciationText": "There's one more way to fail, even with no join at all. Take y equals the cube root of x, at zero. It's unbroken, and it looks smooth. But the secant gradients are h to the power minus two thirds. They grow without limit from both sides, because the tangent at the origin is vertical. A board there would point straight up. A vertical tangent has no gradient, so the cube root of x isn't differentiable at zero. That's why the definition says a tangent that isn't vertical. At a cusp, a sharp point where both sides arrive steeply, the gradients blow up in opposite directions. So there's no derivative there either."
    },
