      "costUsd": 0.011131499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Now a worked example. h of x is x squared for x up to two, and ay x plus b for x greater than two. Find a and b so that h is differentiable at two. Match the gradients first. x squared has gradient two x, which is four at x equals two. The line's gradient is a. So ay equals four. Now make the pieces meet. At x equals two, x squared is four, and the line gives two a plus b. So four equals eight plus b, and b is minus four. The line is y equals four x minus four, the tangent to x squared at two, so a board rolls through without a jolt. You need both equations. The gradient alone leaves b free, and the join could still jump."
    },
